1
00:00:00,540 --> 00:00:03,779
part 2 chapter 2.

2
00:00:03,779 --> 00:00:07,799
Winston picked his way up the lane through dappled light and shade, stepping

3
00:00:07,799 --> 00:00:11,040
out into pools of gold wherever the Bowers parted.

4
00:00:11,040 --> 00:00:15,659
Under the trees to the left of him the ground was misty with bluebells. The air

5
00:00:15,659 --> 00:00:20,700
seemed to kiss one's skin. It was the 2nd of May. From somewhere deeper in the

6
00:00:20,700 --> 00:00:24,540
heart of the wood came the droning of ring dubs

7
00:00:24,540 --> 00:00:28,500
He was a bit early. There had been no difficulties about the journey, and the

8
00:00:28,500 --> 00:00:33,840
girl was so evidently experienced that he was less frightened than he would normally have been.

9
00:00:33,840 --> 00:00:37,379
Presumably she could be trusted to find a safe place.

10
00:00:37,379 --> 00:00:41,579
In general you could not assume that you were much safer in the country than in London.

11
00:00:41,579 --> 00:00:45,180
There were no telescreens, of course, but there was always the danger of concealed

12
00:00:45,180 --> 00:00:48,959
microphones by which your voice might be picked up and recognized

13
00:00:48,959 --> 00:00:53,459
besides, it was not easy to make a journey by yourself without attracting attention.

14
00:00:53,459 --> 00:00:57,120
For distances of less than 100 kilometers it was not necessary to get

15
00:00:57,120 --> 00:01:00,600
your passport endorsed, but sometimes there were patrols hanging about the

16
00:01:00,600 --> 00:01:04,920
railway stations, who examined the papers of any Party member they found there and

17
00:01:04,920 --> 00:01:07,318
asked awkward questions.

18
00:01:07,318 --> 00:01:11,040
However, no patrols had appeared, and on the walk from the station he had made

19
00:01:11,040 --> 00:01:15,120
sure by cautious backward glances that he was not being followed.

20
00:01:15,120 --> 00:01:19,859
The train was full of pearls in holiday mood because of the summary weather.

21
00:01:19,859 --> 00:01:24,120
The wooden seated carriage in which he traveled was filled to overflowing by a

22
00:01:24,120 --> 00:01:28,439
single enormous family, ranging from a toothless great-grandmother to a month

23
00:01:28,439 --> 00:01:33,299
old baby, going out to spend an afternoon with 'in-laws' in the country, and, as they

24
00:01:33,299 --> 00:01:38,040
freely explained to Winston, to get hold of a little black market butter.

25
00:01:38,040 --> 00:01:42,420
The lane widened, and in a minute he came to the footpath she had told him of, a

26
00:01:42,420 --> 00:01:45,659
mere cattle track which plunged between the bushes.

27
00:01:45,659 --> 00:01:49,020
He had no watch, but it could not be 15 yet.

28
00:01:49,020 --> 00:01:52,620
The blue belts were so thick underfoot that it was impossible not to tread on

29
00:01:52,620 --> 00:01:57,420
them. He knelt down and began picking some, partly to pass the time away, but

30
00:01:57,420 --> 00:02:00,420
also from a vague idea that he would like to have a bunch of flowers to offer

31
00:02:00,420 --> 00:02:04,318
the girl when they met. He had got together a big bunch and was smelling

32
00:02:04,318 --> 00:02:08,398
their faint sickly scent when a sound at his back froze him, the unmistakable

33
00:02:08,398 --> 00:02:11,100
crackle of a foot on twigs.

34
00:02:11,100 --> 00:02:16,139
He went on picking bluebells. It was the best thing to do. It might be the girl, or

35
00:02:16,139 --> 00:02:20,700
he might have been followed after all. To look around was to show guilt. He picked

36
00:02:20,700 --> 00:02:25,559
another and another hand fell lightly on his shoulder.

37
00:02:25,559 --> 00:02:27,059
He looked up.

38
00:02:27,059 --> 00:02:28,620
It was the girl.

39
00:02:28,620 --> 00:02:32,760
She shook her head, evidently as a warning that he must keep silent, then,

40
00:02:32,760 --> 00:02:37,020
parted the bushes and quickly led the way along the narrow track into the wood.

41
00:02:37,020 --> 00:02:42,120
Obviously she had been that way before, for she dodged to the boggy bits as though by habit.

42
00:02:42,120 --> 00:02:46,500
Winston followed, still clasping his bunch of flowers. His first feeling was

43
00:02:46,500 --> 00:02:50,459
relief, but as he watched the strong slender body moving in front of him, with

44
00:02:50,459 --> 00:02:53,879
the scarlet sash that was just tight enough to bring out the curve of her

45
00:02:53,879 --> 00:02:58,318
hips, the sense of his own inferiority was heavy upon him.

46
00:02:58,318 --> 00:03:02,100
Even now it seems quite likely that when she turned around and looked at him she

47
00:03:02,100 --> 00:03:04,200
would draw back after all.

48
00:03:04,200 --> 00:03:07,559
The sweetness of the air and the greenness of the leaves daunted him.

49
00:03:07,559 --> 00:03:11,398
Already on the walk from the station the May sunshine had made him feel dirty and

50
00:03:11,398 --> 00:03:17,040
eatilated the creature of indoors, with the dust of London in the pores of his skin.

51
00:03:17,040 --> 00:03:20,219
It occurred to him that till now she had probably never seen him in broad

52
00:03:20,219 --> 00:03:22,079
daylight in the open.

53
00:03:22,079 --> 00:03:24,898
They came to the fallen tree that she had spoken of.

54
00:03:24,898 --> 00:03:28,620
The girl hopped over and forced apart the bushes, in which there did not seem

55
00:03:28,620 --> 00:03:33,540
to be an opening. When Winston followed her, he found that they were in a natural

56
00:03:33,540 --> 00:03:39,540
clearing, a tiny grassy knoll surrounded by tall saplings that shuttered in completely.

57
00:03:39,540 --> 00:03:42,238
The girl stopped and turned.

58
00:03:42,238 --> 00:03:44,700
'Here we are,' she said.

59
00:03:44,700 --> 00:03:47,818
He was facing her at several paces' distance.

60
00:03:47,818 --> 00:03:51,180
As yet he did not dare move nearer to her.

61
00:03:51,180 --> 00:03:54,420
'I didn't want to say anything in the lane,' she went on, 'in case there's a mic

62
00:03:54,420 --> 00:03:55,559
hidden there.

63
00:03:55,559 --> 00:03:58,020
I don't suppose there is, but there could be.

64
00:03:58,020 --> 00:04:03,959
There's always the chance of one of those swine recognizing your voice. We're all right here.'

65
00:04:03,959 --> 00:04:06,779
He still had not the courage to approach her.

66
00:04:06,779 --> 00:04:09,898
'We're all right here?' he repeated stupidly.

67
00:04:09,898 --> 00:04:14,639
'Yes. Look at the trees there were small ashes, which at some

68
00:04:14,639 --> 00:04:18,840
time had been cut down and had sprouted up again into a forest of poles, none of

69
00:04:18,840 --> 00:04:20,879
them thicker than one's wrist.

70
00:04:20,879 --> 00:04:26,040
'There's nothing big enough to hide a mic in. Besides, I've been here before.'

71
00:04:26,040 --> 00:04:31,379
They were only making conversation. He had managed to move closer to her now.

72
00:04:31,379 --> 00:04:35,459
She stood before him very upright, with a smile on her face that looked faintly

73
00:04:35,459 --> 00:04:39,540
ironical, as though she were wondering why he was so slow to act.

74
00:04:39,540 --> 00:04:42,238
The blue bells had cascaded onto the ground.

75
00:04:42,238 --> 00:04:45,120
They seemed to have fallen of their own accord.

76
00:04:45,120 --> 00:04:47,340
He took her hand.

77
00:04:47,340 --> 00:04:52,859
'Would you believe,' he said, 'that till this moment I didn't know what color your eyes were

78
00:04:52,859 --> 00:04:57,719
there were brown, he noted, a rather light shade of brown, with dark lashes.

79
00:04:57,719 --> 00:05:01,799
'Now that you've seen what I'm really like, can you still bear to look at me?'

80
00:05:01,799 --> 00:05:03,779
'Yes, easily.'

81
00:05:03,779 --> 00:05:09,059
'I'm 39 years old. I've got a wife that I can't get rid of. I've got varicose veins.

82
00:05:09,059 --> 00:05:11,398
I've got five false teeth.'

83
00:05:11,398 --> 00:05:14,700
'I couldn't care less,' said the girl.

84
00:05:14,700 --> 00:05:19,559
The next moment, it was hard to say by whozak she was in his arms. At the

85
00:05:19,559 --> 00:05:23,159
beginning he had no feeling except sheer incredulity.

86
00:05:23,159 --> 00:05:26,639
The youthful body was strained against his own, the mass of dark hair was

87
00:05:26,639 --> 00:05:30,540
against his face, and yes! actually she had turned her face up and he was

88
00:05:30,540 --> 00:05:32,639
kissing the wide red mouth.

89
00:05:32,639 --> 00:05:38,579
She had clasped her arms about his neck, she was calling him darling, precious one, loved one.

90
00:05:38,579 --> 00:05:42,719
He had pulled her down onto the ground, she was utterly unresisting, he could do

91
00:05:42,719 --> 00:05:44,159
what he liked with her.

92
00:05:44,159 --> 00:05:48,659
But the truth was that he had no physical sensation, except that of mere contact.

93
00:05:48,659 --> 00:05:53,818
All he felt was incredulity and pride. He was glad that this was happening, but he

94
00:05:53,818 --> 00:05:55,620
had no physical desire.

95
00:05:55,620 --> 00:05:59,818
It was too soon, her youth and prettiness had frightened him, he was too much used

96
00:05:59,818 --> 00:06:03,238
to living without women— —he did not know the reason.

97
00:06:03,238 --> 00:06:06,478
The girl picked herself up and pulled a Blue Bell out of her hair.

98
00:06:06,478 --> 00:06:10,318
She sat against him, putting her arm around his waist.

99
00:06:10,318 --> 00:06:15,000
'Never mind, dear. There's no hurry. We've got the whole afternoon. Isn't this a

100
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:19,920
splendid Hideout I found it when I got lost once on a community hike. If anyone

101
00:06:19,920 --> 00:06:23,398
was coming you could hear them a hundred meters away.'

102
00:06:23,398 --> 00:06:26,219
'What is your name?' said Winston.

103
00:06:26,219 --> 00:06:27,359
'Julia.

104
00:06:27,359 --> 00:06:28,799
I know yours.

105
00:06:28,799 --> 00:06:32,100
It's Winston— —Winston Smith.'

106
00:06:32,100 --> 00:06:35,100
'How did you find that out?'

107
00:06:35,100 --> 00:06:38,398
'I expect I'm better at finding things out than you are, dear.

108
00:06:38,398 --> 00:06:43,079
Tell me, what did you think of me before that day I gave you the note?'

109
00:06:43,079 --> 00:06:47,100
He did not feel any temptation to tell lies to her. It was even a sort of Love

110
00:06:47,100 --> 00:06:49,939
offering to start off by telling the worst.

111
00:06:49,939 --> 00:06:55,439
'I hated the sight of you,' he said. 'I wanted to rape you and then murder you afterwards.

112
00:06:55,439 --> 00:06:59,520
Two weeks ago I thought seriously of smashing your head in with a cobblestone.

113
00:06:59,520 --> 00:07:04,439
If you really want to know, I imagined that you had something to do with the Thought Police.'

114
00:07:04,439 --> 00:07:08,219
The girl laughed delightedly, evidently taking this as a tribute to the

115
00:07:08,219 --> 00:07:10,200
excellence of her disguise.

116
00:07:10,200 --> 00:07:14,219
'Not the Thought Police! You didn't honestly think that?'

117
00:07:14,219 --> 00:07:18,420
'Well, perhaps not exactly that. But from your general appearance—

118
00:07:18,420 --> 00:07:23,340
—merely because you're young and fresh and healthy, you understand— —I thought that probably———'

119
00:07:23,340 --> 00:07:25,738
'You thought I was a good Party member.

120
00:07:25,738 --> 00:07:27,898
Pure in word and deed.

121
00:07:27,898 --> 00:07:32,279
Banners, processions, slogans, games, community hikes— —all that stuff.

122
00:07:32,279 --> 00:07:35,818
And you thought that if I had a quarter of a chance I'd denounce you as a

123
00:07:35,818 --> 00:07:38,398
thought criminal and get you killed off?'

124
00:07:38,398 --> 00:07:42,959
'Yes, something of that kind. A great many young girls are like that, you know.'

125
00:07:42,959 --> 00:07:47,159
'It's this bloody thing that does it,' she said, ripping off the scarlet sash of the

126
00:07:47,159 --> 00:07:52,200
Junior Anti-Sex League and flinging it onto a bow Then, as though touching her waist had

127
00:07:52,200 --> 00:07:55,859
reminded her of something, she felt in the pocket of her overalls and produced

128
00:07:55,859 --> 00:07:58,020
a small slab of chocolate.

129
00:07:58,020 --> 00:08:01,439
She broke it in half and gave one of the pieces to Winston.

130
00:08:01,439 --> 00:08:06,120
Even before he had taken it he knew by the smell that it was very unusual chocolate.

131
00:08:06,120 --> 00:08:09,840
It was dark and shiny, and was wrapped in silver paper.

132
00:08:09,840 --> 00:08:13,620
Chocolate normally was dull Brown crumbly stuff that tasted, as nearly as

133
00:08:13,620 --> 00:08:17,340
one could describe it, like the smoke of a rubbish fire.

134
00:08:17,340 --> 00:08:22,020
But at some time or another he had tasted chocolate like the piece she had given him.

135
00:08:22,020 --> 00:08:25,318
The first whiff of its scent had stirred up some memory which he could not pin

136
00:08:25,318 --> 00:08:28,639
down, but which was powerful and troubling.

137
00:08:28,639 --> 00:08:32,279
'Where did you get this stuff?' he said.

138
00:08:32,279 --> 00:08:34,139
'Black market,' she said indifferently.

139
00:08:34,139 --> 00:08:37,200
'Actually I am that sort of girl, to look at.

140
00:08:37,200 --> 00:08:41,879
I'm good at games. I was a troop leader in the Spies. I do voluntary work three

141
00:08:41,879 --> 00:08:44,398
evenings a week for the Junior anti-sex League.

142
00:08:44,398 --> 00:08:48,359
Hours and hours I've spent pasting their bloody rot all over London. I always

143
00:08:48,359 --> 00:08:52,559
carry one end of a banner in the processions. I always look cheerful and I

144
00:08:52,559 --> 00:08:56,398
never shirk anything. Always yell with the crowd, that's what I say.

145
00:08:56,398 --> 00:08:59,120
It's the only way to be safe.'

146
00:08:59,120 --> 00:09:03,898
The first fragment of chocolate had melted on Winston's tongue. The taste was

147
00:09:03,898 --> 00:09:07,559
delightful. But there was still that memory moving around the edges of his

148
00:09:07,559 --> 00:09:11,939
consciousness, something strongly felt but not reducible to definite shape, like

149
00:09:11,939 --> 00:09:14,579
an object seen out of the corner of one's eye.

150
00:09:14,579 --> 00:09:18,120
He pushed it away from him, aware only that it was the memory of some action

151
00:09:18,120 --> 00:09:22,200
which he would have liked to undo but could not.

152
00:09:22,200 --> 00:09:26,579
'You are very young,' he said. 'You were 10 or 15 years younger than I am.

153
00:09:26,579 --> 00:09:30,000
What could you see to attract you in a man like me?'

154
00:09:30,000 --> 00:09:35,279
'It was something in your face. I thought I'd take a chance. I'm good at spotting

155
00:09:35,279 --> 00:09:40,859
people who don't belong. As soon as I saw you I knew you were against Them,

156
00:09:40,859 --> 00:09:45,420
it appeared, meant the Party, and above all the Inner Party, about whom she

157
00:09:45,420 --> 00:09:49,680
talked with an open jeering hatred which made Winston feel uneasy, although he

158
00:09:49,680 --> 00:09:53,219
knew that they were safe here if they could be safe anywhere.

159
00:09:53,219 --> 00:09:57,000
A thing that astonished him about her was the coarseness of her language.

160
00:09:57,000 --> 00:10:01,020
Party members were supposed not to swear, and Winston himself very seldom did

161
00:10:01,020 --> 00:10:03,238
swear, aloud, at any rate.

162
00:10:03,238 --> 00:10:07,318
Julia, however, seemed unable to mention the Party, and especially the Inner Party,

163
00:10:07,318 --> 00:10:11,879
without using the kind of words that you saw chalked up in dripping alleyways. He

164
00:10:11,879 --> 00:10:15,659
did not dislike it. It was merely one symptom of her revolt against the Party

165
00:10:15,659 --> 00:10:20,219
in all its ways, and somehow it seemed natural and healthy, like the sneeze of a

166
00:10:20,219 --> 00:10:22,439
horse that smells bad hay.

167
00:10:22,439 --> 00:10:25,379
They had left the clearing and were wandering again through the checkered

168
00:10:25,379 --> 00:10:28,500
shade, with their arms around each other's waists whenever it was wide

169
00:10:28,500 --> 00:10:32,579
enough to walk to a breast He noticed how much softer her waist

170
00:10:32,579 --> 00:10:35,159
seemed to feel now that the sash was gone.

171
00:10:35,159 --> 00:10:37,680
They did not speak above a whisper.

172
00:10:37,680 --> 00:10:41,879
Outside the clearing, Julia said, it was better to go quietly.

173
00:10:41,879 --> 00:10:46,379
Presently they had reached the edge of the little wood. She stopped him.

174
00:10:46,379 --> 00:10:50,219
'Don't go out into the open. There might be someone watching. We're all right if

175
00:10:50,219 --> 00:10:54,659
we keep behind the bows They were standing in the shade of hazel

176
00:10:54,659 --> 00:11:00,059
bushes. The sunlight, filtering through innumerable leaves, was still hot on their faces.

177
00:11:00,059 --> 00:11:05,818
Winston looked out into the field beyond, and underwent a curious, slow shock of recognition.

178
00:11:05,818 --> 00:11:11,398
He knew it by sight. An old, close-bitten pasture, with a footpath wandering across

179
00:11:11,398 --> 00:11:13,738
it and a mold Hill here and there.

180
00:11:13,738 --> 00:11:18,000
In the ragged hedge on the opposite side the bowels of the elm trees swayed just

181
00:11:18,000 --> 00:11:23,760
perceptibly in the breeze, and their leaves stirred faintly in dense masses like women's hair.

182
00:11:23,760 --> 00:11:27,540
Surely somewhere nearby, but out of sight, there must be a stream with green pools

183
00:11:27,540 --> 00:11:30,120
where days were swimming?

184
00:11:30,120 --> 00:11:31,920
'Isn't there a stream somewhere near here?'

185
00:11:31,920 --> 00:11:33,238
he whispered.

186
00:11:33,238 --> 00:11:37,680
'That's right, there is a stream. It's at the edge of the next field, actually

187
00:11:37,680 --> 00:11:41,760
they're efficient great big ones. You can watch them lying in the pools under the

188
00:11:41,760 --> 00:11:44,520
willow trees, waving their tails.'

189
00:11:44,520 --> 00:11:48,180
'It's the Golden Country— —almost,' he murmured.

190
00:11:48,180 --> 00:11:50,219
'The Golden Country?'

191
00:11:50,219 --> 00:11:54,659
'It's nothing, really. A landscape I've seen sometimes in a dream.'

192
00:11:54,659 --> 00:11:57,180
'Look!' whispered Julia.

193
00:11:57,180 --> 00:12:01,260
A thrusher delighted on a bow not five meters away, almost at the level of their

194
00:12:01,260 --> 00:12:04,020
faces. Perhaps it had not seen them.

195
00:12:04,020 --> 00:12:06,600
It was in the sun, they in the shade.

196
00:12:06,600 --> 00:12:10,318
It spread out its wings, fitted them carefully into place again, ducked its

197
00:12:10,318 --> 00:12:14,340
head for a moment, as though making a sort of obeisance to the sun, and then

198
00:12:14,340 --> 00:12:17,340
began to pour forth a torrent of song.

199
00:12:17,340 --> 00:12:20,879
In the afternoon hush the volume of sound was startling.

200
00:12:20,879 --> 00:12:23,879
Winston and Julia clung together, fascinated.

201
00:12:23,879 --> 00:12:28,379
The music went on and on, minute after minute, with astonishing variations, never

202
00:12:28,379 --> 00:12:33,478
once repeating itself, almost as if the bird were deliberately showing off its virtuosity.

203
00:12:33,478 --> 00:12:37,680
Sometimes it stopped for a few seconds, spread out and resettled its wings, then

204
00:12:37,680 --> 00:12:41,219
swelled its speckled breast and again burst into song.

205
00:12:41,219 --> 00:12:44,279
Winston watched it with a sort of vague reverence.

206
00:12:44,279 --> 00:12:46,738
For whom, for what, was that bird singing?

207
00:12:46,738 --> 00:12:51,180
No mate, no rival was watching it. What made it sit at the edge of the lonely

208
00:12:51,180 --> 00:12:54,359
wood and pour its music into nothingness?

209
00:12:54,359 --> 00:12:58,139
He wondered whether after all there was a microphone hidden somewhere near.

210
00:12:58,139 --> 00:13:01,978
He and Julia had spoken only in low whispers, and it would not pick up what

211
00:13:01,978 --> 00:13:05,100
they had said, but it would pick up the thrush.

212
00:13:05,100 --> 00:13:09,779
Perhaps at the other end of the instrument some small, beetle-like man was listening intently—

213
00:13:09,779 --> 00:13:13,738
—listening to that by degrees the flood of music drove all

214
00:13:13,738 --> 00:13:17,520
speculations out of his mind. It was as though it were a kind of liquid stuff

215
00:13:17,520 --> 00:13:22,379
that poured all over him and got mixed up with the sunlight that filtered through the leaves.

216
00:13:22,379 --> 00:13:24,959
He stopped thinking and merely felt.

217
00:13:24,959 --> 00:13:28,439
The girl's waist in the bend of his arm was soft and warm.

218
00:13:28,439 --> 00:13:34,020
He pulled her around so that they were breast to breast; her body seemed to melt into his.

219
00:13:34,020 --> 00:13:37,680
Wherever his hands moved it was all his yielding as water.

220
00:13:37,680 --> 00:13:41,639
Their mouths clung together; it was quite different from the hard kisses they had

221
00:13:41,639 --> 00:13:43,318
exchanged earlier.

222
00:13:43,318 --> 00:13:47,760
When they moved their faces apart again both of them sighed deeply.

223
00:13:47,760 --> 00:13:51,299
The birds took fright and fled with a clatter of wings.

224
00:13:51,299 --> 00:13:53,939
Winston put his lips against her ear.

225
00:13:53,939 --> 00:13:56,159
'Now, he whispered.

226
00:13:56,159 --> 00:14:00,719
'Not here,' she whispered back. 'Come back to the Hideout It's safer.'

227
00:14:00,719 --> 00:14:04,079
Quickly, with an occasional crackle of twigs, they threaded their way back to

228
00:14:04,079 --> 00:14:09,000
the clearing. When they were once inside the ring of saplings she turned and faced him.

229
00:14:09,000 --> 00:14:14,639
They were both breathing fast, but the smile had reappeared round the corners of her mouth.

230
00:14:14,639 --> 00:14:18,898
She stood looking at him for an instant, then felt at the zipper of her overalls.

231
00:14:18,898 --> 00:14:21,719
And, yes! it was almost as in his dream.

232
00:14:21,719 --> 00:14:25,920
Almost as swiftly as he had imagined it, she had torn her clothes off, and when

233
00:14:25,920 --> 00:14:29,459
she flung them aside it was with that same magnificent gesture by which a

234
00:14:29,459 --> 00:14:33,000
whole civilization seemed to be annihilated.

235
00:14:33,000 --> 00:14:37,559
Her body gleamed white in the sun. But for a moment he did not look at her body;

236
00:14:37,559 --> 00:14:42,120
his eyes were anchored by the freckled face with its faint, bold smile.

237
00:14:42,120 --> 00:14:46,318
He knelt down before her and took her hands in his.

238
00:14:46,318 --> 00:14:48,840
'Have you done this before?'

239
00:14:48,840 --> 00:14:53,159
'Of course. Hundreds of times— —well, scores of times, anyway.'

240
00:14:53,159 --> 00:14:57,238
'With Party members?' 'Yes, always with Party members.'

241
00:14:57,238 --> 00:14:59,700
'With members of the Inner Party?'

242
00:14:59,700 --> 00:15:03,719
'Not with those why no. But there's plenty that would if they got half a chance.

243
00:15:03,719 --> 00:15:07,200
They're not so holy as they make out.'

244
00:15:07,200 --> 00:15:09,120
His heart leapt.

245
00:15:09,120 --> 00:15:14,100
Scores of times she had done it: he wished it had been hundreds— —thousands.

246
00:15:14,100 --> 00:15:17,818
Anything that hinted at corruption always filled him with a wild hope.

247
00:15:17,818 --> 00:15:21,180
Who knew, perhaps the Party was rotten under the surface, its cult of

248
00:15:21,180 --> 00:15:25,318
strenuousness and self-denial simply a sham concealing iniquity.

249
00:15:25,318 --> 00:15:28,799
If he could have infected the whole lot of them with leprosy or syphilis, how

250
00:15:28,799 --> 00:15:30,540
gladly he would have done so!

251
00:15:30,540 --> 00:15:33,420
Anything to rot, to weaken, to undermine!

252
00:15:33,420 --> 00:15:37,500
He pulled her down so that they were kneeling face to face.

253
00:15:37,500 --> 00:15:42,299
'Listen. The more men you've had, the more I love you. Do you understand that?'

254
00:15:42,299 --> 00:15:44,159
'Yes, perfectly.'

255
00:15:44,159 --> 00:15:49,379
'I hate purity, I hate goodness! I don't want any virtue to exist anywhere. I want

256
00:15:49,379 --> 00:15:52,200
everyone to be corrupt to the bones.'

257
00:15:52,200 --> 00:15:56,100
'Well then, I ought to suit you, dear. I'm corrupt to the bones.'

258
00:15:56,100 --> 00:16:00,540
'You like doing this? I don't mean simply me: I mean the thing in itself?'

259
00:16:00,540 --> 00:16:02,879
'I adore it.'

260
00:16:02,879 --> 00:16:05,279
That was above all what he wanted to hear.

261
00:16:05,279 --> 00:16:10,978
Not merely the love of one person, but the animal instinct, the simple undifferentiated desire:

262
00:16:10,978 --> 00:16:15,420
that was the force that would tear the Party to pieces. He pressed her down upon

263
00:16:15,420 --> 00:16:17,520
the grass, among the fallen bluebells.

264
00:16:17,520 --> 00:16:19,279
This time there was no difficulty.

265
00:16:19,279 --> 00:16:23,760
Presently the rising and falling of their breasts slowed to normal speed, and

266
00:16:23,760 --> 00:16:26,639
in a sort of pleasant helplessness they fell apart.

267
00:16:26,639 --> 00:16:30,659
The sun seemed to have grown hotter. They were both sleepy.

268
00:16:30,659 --> 00:16:34,439
He reached out for the discarded overalls and pulled them partly over her.

269
00:16:34,439 --> 00:16:39,079
Almost immediately they fell asleep and slept for about half an hour.

270
00:16:39,079 --> 00:16:43,559
Winston worked first. He sat up and watched the freckled face, still

271
00:16:43,559 --> 00:16:46,920
peacefully asleep, pillowed on the palm of her hand.

272
00:16:46,920 --> 00:16:49,859
Except for her mouth, you could not call her beautiful.

273
00:16:49,859 --> 00:16:53,879
There was a line or two around the eyes, if you looked closely. The short dark

274
00:16:53,879 --> 00:16:55,799
hair was extraordinarily thick and soft.

275
00:16:55,799 --> 00:17:00,959
It occurred to him that he still did not know her surname or where she lived.

276
00:17:00,959 --> 00:17:07,260
The young, strong body, now helpless in sleep awoken him a pitying, protecting feeling.

277
00:17:07,260 --> 00:17:10,978
But the mindless tenderness that he had felt under the hazel tree, while the

278
00:17:10,978 --> 00:17:13,799
thrush was singing, had not quite come back.

279
00:17:13,799 --> 00:17:17,639
He pulled the overalls aside and studied her smooth white flank.

280
00:17:17,639 --> 00:17:21,059
In the old days, he thought, a man looked at a girl's body and saw that it was

281
00:17:21,059 --> 00:17:23,639
desirable, and that was the end of the story.

282
00:17:23,639 --> 00:17:28,260
But you could not have pure love or pure lust nowadays. No emotion was pure,

283
00:17:28,260 --> 00:17:31,079
because everything was mixed up with fear and hatred.

284
00:17:31,079 --> 00:17:34,500
Their embrace had been a battle, the climax of victory.

285
00:17:34,500 --> 00:17:36,299
It was a blow struck against the Party.

286
00:17:36,299 --> 00:17:39,000
It was a political act
